% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
In Content-Centric Vehicular Networks (CCVNs), proactive RSU caching requires accurate prediction of vehicle dwell time at the moment a content request is issued.
Prior work on vehicular mobility prediction relies on continuous time-series inputs and is not suited to the event-driven, single-snapshot inference setting at an RSU.
This paper proposes \textbf{H-ST-MBAN} (Hybrid Spatio-Temporal Multi-Branch Attention Network), a fully deterministic regression model for RSU dwell time prediction.
H-ST-MBAN decomposes input features into three semantically distinct branches: Kinematic (K), Traffic Control (T), and Social/Contextual (S).
Each branch is encoded independently by a dedicated Residual Block (ResBlock) to prevent gradient conflict across heterogeneous variable groups.
Cyclical encoding resolves signal-phase discontinuities in the traffic control branch, and a three-token multi-head self-attention layer subsequently integrates the branch representations into a unified tensor.
The RSU utilizes this unified tensor to compute the expected residency period for the requesting vehicle.
H-ST-MBAN incorporates an XGBoost-based tabular prior stream to model sharp decision boundaries, while the neural network branches learn spatio-temporal residual errors.
The two streams are fused via a learnable gating mechanism for final regression.
Twelve SUMO-derived microscopic variables augment the feature set with fine-grained local traffic dynamics not available from GPS-level mobility traces.
The predecessor model ST-CVAE employed a Conditional Variational Autoencoder (CVAE); analysis shows that the conditional dwell-time distribution is effectively unimodal given a sufficiently informative feature vector.
This effectively unimodal characteristic causes the CVAE latent variable to collapse toward a deterministic mapping without distributional benefit.
H-ST-MBAN eliminates this overhead by adopting explicit deterministic regression, improving point-estimate accuracy while reducing inference latency.
Experiments on SUMO-simulated CCVN environments confirm that H-ST-MBAN achieves lower MAE and RMSE than ST-CVAE and representative ML/DL baselines.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Content-Centric Vehicular Networks, RSU Dwell Time Prediction,
Hybrid Multi-Branch Attention Network, Snapshot-Based Inference,
Vehicle-to-Infrastructure, Edge Caching, Microscopic Traffic Simulation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\IEEEpeerreviewmaketitle


% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
% ============================================================

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} advancement of the Internet of Vehicles (IoV) and connected vehicles has established road-side infrastructure as an active participant in content distribution.
In Content-Centric Vehicular Networks, Road-Side Units (RSUs) act as edge caching nodes to deliver requested content via Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) links \cite{1,3,45}.
Because a vehicle typically traverses an RSU communication zone within seconds to tens of seconds, content unavailable at the moment of a request cannot always be fully transmitted before the vehicle exits the coverage area.
The V2I communication channel provides the fundamental medium through which content chunks are continuously delivered to moving vehicles.
Consequently, proactive caching---placing content at the RSU before demand materializes---is essential to maintain service continuity across consecutive intersections \cite{32,33,39}.

The effectiveness of proactive caching strategies relies heavily on estimating how long a particular vehicle will remain within the RSU coverage zone.
Without an accurate estimate of this dwell time at the moment a content request is issued, a precaching scheduler cannot determine whether sufficient time exists to complete a transfer.
The scheduler cannot effectively prioritize among competing requests when available V2I bandwidth is constrained.
Such scheduling decisions must be executed instantly upon the arrival of a V2I request packet to prevent under-utilization of the available communication window.
Accurate, low-latency prediction of RSU dwell time in an event-driven setting is therefore a foundational requirement for CCVN precaching systems \cite{42,51}.

However, existing approaches face several critical limitations regarding their applicability to CCVN operations.
First, most vehicular mobility prediction methods \cite{41,43} rely on continuous time-series inputs from globally collected sensor data.
These approaches are structurally misaligned with the event-driven, single-snapshot inference environment of an RSU.
When a request arrives, the RSU possesses only the instantaneous state of the vehicle without historical sequences, making continuous tracking via V2I uplink transmissions inefficient.
Adapting sequence-based architectures to this setting introduces buffering delays, consumes bandwidth that should be reserved for content delivery, and degrades overall prediction accuracy.
Second, prior studies on proactive caching \cite{47,48,49} focus primarily on content popularity modeling, often failing to estimate the explicit scalar dwell time required to schedule individual transfers.
Third, existing methods frequently overlook the practical constraints of edge hardware by implicitly assuming centralized aggregation and inference using powerful servers \cite{61,64}.
In a realistic CCVN, predictive models must operate within the limited computational and memory budgets of local RSUs, without relying on continuous cloud connectivity.

\IEEEpubidadjcol
To address these challenges, we propose \textbf{H-ST-MBAN}, a deterministic dwell-time predictor designed for event-driven, RSU-local inference.
H-ST-MBAN captures a single snapshot of the vehicle state at the moment of a request, processing it immediately without historical buffering.
The inference relies solely on the data payload embedded within the initial content request packet and local RSU sensor readings.
To enable continuous local adaptation, we introduce a Dual-Model fine-tuning architecture where a globally pre-trained base model is deployed to the RSU.
The RSU then fine-tunes a shadow model using a local snapshot queue, alternating roles with the active inference model to adapt to intersection-specific traffic dynamics without service interruption.
Unlike our predecessor model ST-CVAE, which experienced posterior collapse due to the effectively unimodal nature of conditional dwell-time distributions, H-ST-MBAN adopts an explicit deterministic regression architecture.
This design eliminates unnecessary Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence training overhead and inference sampling latency, streamlining the model for efficient RSU deployment without sacrificing distributional expressiveness.

The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows.
First, we replace the CVAE formulation of ST-CVAE with a fully deterministic regression architecture for dwell-time estimation.
We provide theoretical justification via posterior collapse analysis and empirical evidence from the ST-CVAE equivalence to a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) at convergence.
Second, we partition the input feature space into three semantically distinct branches: Kinematic (K), Traffic Control (T), and Social/Contextual (S).
Each branch is encoded by a dedicated ResBlock and fused via multi-head self-attention into a deep ResNet.
This decomposition prevents gradient conflicts across heterogeneous variable groups and allows each encoder to apply processing suited to the statistical structure of its inputs.
Third, we introduce a multi-head self-attention mechanism over branch-level representations, allowing the model to aggregate branch contributions according to the current traffic context.
Fourth, we incorporate an XGBoost-based tabular prior stream to capture sharp decision boundaries inherent to vehicular datasets.
The spatio-temporal neural network branches focus on learning residual errors, and the two streams are combined using a learnable gating fusion mechanism.
Fifth, we augment the input feature set with twelve variables obtained from the SUMO microscopic traffic simulator.
These variables capture fine-grained local dynamics including leader-follower gaps, lane occupancy, and intersection queue lengths that are unavailable from GPS-level mobility traces alone.
Sixth, we mitigate the degradation of model weight parameters caused by outlier variables during local adaptation by incorporating a steady-state filtering mechanism that stabilizes gradient updates.
Seventh, we resolve the limitations of tree ensembles like XGBoost that operate with static global parameters at edge nodes by enabling local fine-tuning to continuously track changing spatial-temporal vehicular dynamics.
Finally, to ensure service continuity during local adaptation, we propose a ping-pong update strategy where the RSU fine-tunes a shadow model using a local snapshot queue while a primary model serves requests.
This ensures uninterrupted adaptation to intersection-specific traffic patterns without blocking real-time inference.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work on vehicular caching, mobility prediction, and dwell-time estimation.
Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes the system model, defines the prediction task, and enumerates the input feature space for the communications scenario.
Section~\ref{sec:architecture} describes the H-ST-MBAN architecture and the underlying V2I data exchange protocols in detail.
Section~\ref{sec:experiments} presents the simulation environment, dataset collection procedure, and experimental results validating the proposed framework.
Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper with a discussion on future integration of decentralized learning models in CCVNs.


% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}
% ============================================================

We survey prior work along six key dimensions relevant to RSU-based proactive caching. These dimensions include the comparison of network architectures between content-centric IoV and conventional vehicle-to-infrastructure systems, and the categorization of caching triggers as either reactive or proactive. Furthermore, we differentiate studies based on their dwell-time awareness, contrasting explicit sojourn estimations against implicit models, and classify their mobility inputs as either continuous traces or single-snapshot observations. Finally, we analyze the prediction targets, distinguishing content popularity from vehicle sojourn times, and evaluate inference latency by separating online-training requirements from pre-trained inference-only frameworks.
Table~\ref{tab:related_work} summarizes representative works across these dimensions.
\paragraph{Category 1: CIoV and Content-Centric Networking}
Content-Centric IoV (CIoV) embeds Named Data Networking semantics into vehicular architectures,
allowing RSUs and vehicles to cache and forward content by name rather than address \cite{1,3,5}.
Recent work has extended CIoV to edge-computing and mobility-aware routing contexts,
improving content availability under dynamic topologies \cite{8,10,11}.
\paragraph{Category 2: V2I Precaching}
V2I precaching exploits scheduled RSU-to-vehicle links to deliver content before a request is issued.
Federated learning approaches \cite{17,18,20} distribute model training across vehicles to protect privacy
while adapting caching decisions to real-time traffic conditions.
Age-of-Information and cooperative strategies further refine delivery timing under bandwidth constraints \cite{15}.
\paragraph{Category 3: Popularity-Based and Hybrid Precaching}
Popularity-based schemes predict which content items will be requested and prefetch them to edge nodes \cite{32,33,34}.
Hybrid designs combine popularity estimation with context signals, such as social ties or unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) relay paths,
improving hit rates in heterogeneous topologies \cite{47,48,49}.
\paragraph{Category 4: Mobility Prediction and Combined Caching}
Mobility-aware caching couples vehicle trajectory forecasts with content placement decisions \cite{39,42,43}.
These methods commonly use multi-step time-series models (e.g., LSTM, Transformer variants) to predict
future RSU associations, subsequently pre-positioning content along predicted paths \cite{41,45}.
\paragraph{Category 5: RSU-Local and Snapshot-Based Learning}
A growing body of work deploys lightweight inference directly at the RSU using only locally available
observations, avoiding the need for continuous uplink traces \cite{64,68,71}.
Federated and hierarchical learning frameworks have been proposed to aggregate RSU-local models
while preserving vehicle privacy \cite{60,61,67}.
However, these methods still assume either repeated interactions or cumulative observation windows.
To our knowledge, no existing method performs deterministic dwell-time regression from a single kinematic snapshot at the instant of a content request, which is the operating condition targeted in this work.

